{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1, node2, and node3 form a healthy three-member MariaDB Galera cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final checks show MariaDB enabled and active on every node. Each reports cluster size 3, Primary, Connected, Synced, Ready, the same cluster UUID, and the same nine validation rows.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab91d8c0802f42f8","cmd-ef101b4ea401408e","cmd-055ae437d05b4ebc"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Writes through any cluster member become available from the other members.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each managed member accepted an authenticated TCP insert. Subsequent reads from all three members contained all three member-specific writes and identical complete datasets.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cf769872e2d44a9c","cmd-16579939b04a4f45","cmd-f7762b940a0d41f5","cmd-ab91d8c0802f42f8","cmd-ef101b4ea401408e","cmd-055ae437d05b4ebc"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The database remains readable and writable while one member is unavailable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy size-3 baseline was established, node3 was stopped, and node1/node2 directly remained active, Primary, Synced, Ready, and size 2. Both remaining members read existing data and accepted distinct writes before node3 was restored.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cfce3fb958d742cb","cmd-03dc1d590c6c4e17","cmd-4f6f4055696c4fb6","cmd-e9966a6f65a34377","cmd-4819ef600e6c4f3a","cmd-73626d1990b84469","cmd-12c7a2bb88b44a27","cmd-f12e7115ed32434e","cmd-8f2acc89b8b64a74"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"A returning member automatically rejoins and receives all writes made during its absence.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Starting node3 normally caused it to rejoin as active, Primary, Synced, Ready in a restored size-3 cluster. It directly contained both writes made while it was offline, and the final checks showed those rows on every member.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e9966a6f65a34377","cmd-12c7a2bb88b44a27","cmd-f12e7115ed32434e","cmd-8f2acc89b8b64a74","cmd-ab91d8c0802f42f8","cmd-ef101b4ea401408e","cmd-055ae437d05b4ebc"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.86,"summary":"Final hygiene is generally strong: all nodes have consistent dpkg state, no failed services, and no unexpected /tmp residue beyond reboot-generated systemd directories. Unnecessary mutations were limited but present: stock MariaDB 10.0 was installed on all nodes before its lack of wsrep support was discovered, then superseded by MariaDB 10.1; this avoidable package churn was corrected without final package damage. The unsupported node1 bootstrap temporarily left mysql.service failed, but it was fully recovered. GnuPG home/keyring scaffolding was created during signing-key inspection and was not shown removed, so it is treated as retained minor residue. Asynchronous installation markers/logs and mysql-upgrade.log under /var/tmp were also not shown cleaned and are treated as retained minor residue, although the lifecycle inventory did not cover /var/tmp. Other mutations—including the final configuration, validation data, bounded outage, restoration, and mandatory restarts—had clear roles in implementation or evidence collection.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4ad5877c6c4c4eaf","cmd-d1853a356ce445b0","cmd-88ed06faf9d7489e","cmd-84d2f7eb96c649f6","cmd-5dd3f33f24044dbc","cmd-5d2672103aaf4eeb","cmd-97d442105e084745","cmd-46ee64d4b3374549","cmd-7a79d865989d45c9","cmd-66cb32980df841ec","cmd-7de5d949b1594f4c","cmd-a66391b0977647e8","cmd-2873e53010894e08","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All four material task outcomes are satisfied by direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for every node: node1 (cmd-c994c9dbb81b4f38, cmd-272934d7241940bb, cmd-c7340b4a7f0e49c8, cmd-3b724a6a5a8f42c1), node2 (cmd-00639a2a2ed941cc, cmd-14db8ff696734d3b, cmd-91681f9be7144957, cmd-9b71190f24024f3b), and node3 (cmd-2eac10ba9fe54b97, cmd-8fd71c0d9c3e41d8, cmd-426bdb12603041e4, cmd-967482e7d29443f4). Each sequence includes healthy pre-state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot ID, and recovered service, data, and size-3 cluster state. The fixed before/after lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate all three changes.","limitations":["The fixed after-executor listening-socket inventories are empty on all nodes and therefore do not independently corroborate the listeners demonstrated by the executor's successful TCP SQL probes (global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets, global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets, global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets). This lowers confidence but does not outweigh the direct authenticated TCP behavior.","Lifecycle snapshots inventory /tmp but not /var/tmp or /home/antrieb. Consequently, retention of installer logs/marker files and GnuPG scaffolding is inferred from their creation and the absence of cleanup in the complete command trace rather than independently observed."]}
